WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.900
AWQ.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:04.355
Activation-aware weight quantization.

3
00:00:04.355 --> 00:00:07.967
A small group of weights does a surprising amount of the work.

4
00:00:09.167 --> 00:00:14.633
AWQ starts with an observation: look at the activations flowing through the model.

5
00:00:14.933 --> 00:00:16.432
Most channels are quiet.

6
00:00:16.432 --> 00:00:20.367
But roughly one percent carry disproportionately large signals.

7
00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:23.400
Quantize those carelessly and quality craters.

8
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.500
So AWQ protects them.

9
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.533
First it finds the salient channels by activation magnitude.

10
00:00:30.833 --> 00:00:33.000
Then it scales them up before quantization.

11
00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.700
It compensates for that scaling downstream.

12
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.967
Finally the four bit rounding error lands on weights that barely matter.

13
00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:43.167
How does it compare to GPTQ?

14
00:00:43.467 --> 00:00:47.894
GPTQ minimizes layer output error on calibration data.

15
00:00:47.894 --> 00:00:50.600
AWQ protects the salient weights.

16
00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:54.667
At the same bit width, AWQ generally keeps more quality.

17
00:00:54.967 --> 00:00:57.198
But the real decision is empirical.

18
00:00:57.198 --> 00:00:58.600
Run both on your eval.

19
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:04.167
AWQ is a first class citizen in serving stacks like vLLM.

20
00:01:04.467 --> 00:01:09.733
We point at an AWQ checkpoint from the Hub, along with a maximum context length.

21
00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:12.400
And one flag tells vLLM to use it.

22
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.367
Before locking the serving image, run a bake-off.

23
00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:20.733
Deploy the same model as GPTQ and as AWQ.

24
00:01:21.033 --> 00:01:24.567
Run the flight ops eval on both, and log scores and speed.

25
00:01:24.867 --> 00:01:28.216
AWQ wins by four points at identical latency.

26
00:01:28.216 --> 00:01:31.267
Now it's a row in a table you can defend.

27
00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:33.242
Let's recap.

28
00:01:33.242 --> 00:01:36.733
About one percent of channels carry large activations.

29
00:01:37.033 --> 00:01:40.433
AWQ protects them before rounding to four bits.

30
00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:43.633
It usually keeps more quality than GPTQ.

31
00:01:43.933 --> 00:01:46.461
Pick the winner on your own eval.

32
00:01:46.461 --> 00:01:47.533
Next up: GGUF.
